---
import TerminalLayout from '../../layouts/TerminalLayout.astro';
import FloatVisualizer from '../../components/FloatVisualizer.svelte';

const cases = [
  { e: '0', f: '0', kind: 'Zero', value: '(−1)ˢ × 0' },
  { e: '0', f: '≠ 0', kind: 'Subnormal', value: '(−1)ˢ × 0.f × 2⁻¹²⁶' },
  { e: '1 – 254', f: 'any', kind: 'Normal', value: '(−1)ˢ × 1.f × 2⁽ᵉ⁻¹²⁷⁾' },
  { e: '255', f: '0', kind: 'Infinity', value: '(−1)ˢ × ∞' },
  { e: '255', f: '≠ 0', kind: 'NaN', value: 'not a number' },
];
---

<TerminalLayout
  title="Floating Point Internals"
  description="An interactive IEEE 754 binary32 visualizer. Toggle the 32 bits or type a value to see its sign, exponent, fraction and exact stored value."
  image="/og-ieee-754.png"
  imageAlt="The 32 bits of the number 0.1 in IEEE 754 single precision, grouped into sign, exponent and fraction"
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center text-xs font-mono text-zinc-400 hover:text-white mb-8 transition-colors">
      <svg class="w-3 h-3 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path></svg>
      cd ..
    </a>

    <header class="mb-12">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Floating Point Internals</h1>
      <p class="text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
        How a computer stores a real number in 32 bits, using the
        <strong class="text-white">IEEE 754 binary32</strong> (single-precision) format.
        Toggle bits, or type a value, and watch the sign, exponent and fraction fields change.
        Try <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">0.1</code>: the number stored is not exactly one tenth.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mb-12">
      <FloatVisualizer client:load />
    </div>

    <section class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-white font-mono text-sm border-b border-zinc-800 pb-2">UNDER THE HOOD</h2>

      <div class="bg-zinc-900/50 p-6 rounded-lg border border-zinc-800 font-mono text-xs text-zinc-400 space-y-2">
        <p>Memory: 32 bits (4 bytes), laid out left to right:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-4 space-y-1">
          <li><span class="text-red-400">Bit 31:</span> sign (0 = positive, 1 = negative)</li>
          <li><span class="text-green-400">Bits 30–23:</span> exponent field, 8 bits, stored with a bias of 127</li>
          <li><span class="text-blue-400">Bits 22–0:</span> fraction field, 23 bits (the standard calls it the trailing significand field)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left font-mono text-xs border border-zinc-800">
          <caption class="sr-only">How the exponent field and fraction field determine the meaning of a binary32 value</caption>
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Exponent field (e)</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Fraction (f)</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Kind</th>
              <th scope="col" class="p-3">Value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-zinc-400">
            {cases.map(row => (
              <tr class="border-t border-zinc-800">
                <td class="p-3">{row.e}</td>
                <td class="p-3">{row.f}</td>
                <td class="p-3">{row.kind}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400">{row.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="text-sm text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed space-y-4">
        <p>
          The formula <code class="font-mono text-emerald-400">(−1)ˢ × 1.f × 2⁽ᵉ⁻¹²⁷⁾</code> describes only the
          <em>normal</em> numbers. The leading 1 is not stored; it is implied, which is how 23 stored bits give 24 bits
          of precision. When the exponent field is 0, the implied bit becomes 0 and the exponent is fixed at −126, so the
          numbers fade toward zero in even steps (gradual underflow) instead of jumping from the smallest normal number
          straight to 0. When the exponent field is 255, the value is an infinity or a NaN.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most decimal fractions, 0.1 among them, have no finite binary expansion, so the nearest representable value is
          stored instead. For 0.1 that value is 0.100000001490116119384765625. The panel under the bit grid shows the
          exact stored value so you can see the gap.
        </p>
        <p>
          A note on words: this field is often called the <em>mantissa</em>, and an earlier version of this
          page used that word. IEEE 754 uses <em>significand</em> for the whole 1.f quantity and <em>trailing significand field</em>
          for the stored 23 bits, because "mantissa" also names the fractional part of a logarithm, which is a different thing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</TerminalLayout>
